# Production readiness — @@PROJECT@@

This scaffold is a starting point, not production certification. Keep a named
owner, environment, release commit/image digest, date and supporting artifacts
for every check below. Record actual evidence, never copy example successes.

## Required review

- Identity and access: verify login, expired credentials, denied roles, CSRF,
  session cookie settings and admin isolation using the deployed identity system.
- Secrets and TLS: secret manager delivery, rotation, least privilege, HTTPS,
  trusted proxy handling, network isolation and no credentials in logs or images.
- Dependencies: run CI audits and review findings for this exact image/lock set.
- Restore drill: restore a recent encrypted backup into an isolated environment;
  verify data integrity and record measured RPO/RTO against agreed objectives.
- Migrations: rehearse against a representative restored database, review locks,
  duration, disk growth, multiple heads and compatibility with the previous app.
- Deployment rollback: retain the last healthy revision and immutable image digest;
  rehearse restoring traffic, checking health and user flows, with a named owner.
- Monitoring: verify trace/log delivery, cardinality and redaction, alert routing,
  on-call ownership and actionable error/latency thresholds.
- Performance: measure representative requests, concurrency, percentiles, error
  rate and resource use in staging. Define SLOs before using a pass/fail budget.
- Tests: unit, contracts, integration and real user-flow E2E for the release.
- Release approval: reviewer, release identity, rollback criteria and change window.
- AI if enabled: representative domain dataset, task quality, unsafe-input cases,
  refusals, privacy, latency and cost. Fake evals only test the evaluation plumbing.

`harness readiness --project .` inventories `.harness/readiness/<check>.json`
and doctor findings in read-only mode. Required record keys: `schema_version: 1`,
`check`, `status` (`pass` or `fail`), `reviewer`, `scope`, `recorded_at`,
`manifest_sha256` (SHA-256 of `.harness/scaffold.json`). Timestamps must have a
timezone and be within the previous 30 days; this is an inventory freshness
limit, not a guarantee that evidence remains valid after an environment change.
Check IDs: identity_access, secrets_tls, dependency_audit, restore_drill,
migration_rehearsal, deployment_rollback, monitoring_alerts, performance,
tests_contracts_e2e, release_approval, and ai_quality when AI is configured.
Include artifact references and release identity in scope; use an ISO date/time.
These are operator attestations: the CLI cannot verify their truth, currency or
coverage. Even a complete inventory requires independent review and never
authorizes production. Missing or failed records block readiness.

## Backup and migration safety

PostgreSQL: choose logical dump, physical backup/PITR or managed backup according
to the agreed RPO/RTO. Encrypt and restrict backups; retain keys, roles and required
extensions. Test restore with compatible tooling and a separate target. A successful
backup command alone does not prove recoverability. SQLite: use its backup API or
a consistent stopped snapshot; copying an active database file alone is insufficient.
Memory adapters have no durable data and must not be used for durable workloads.

Before migration: review the revision graph and generated SQL where supported;
take and verify a recoverable backup; rehearse on a restored staging copy; estimate
locks and duration; arrange an abort threshold and maintenance window if needed.
Use expand/contract changes: add compatible schema, deploy/backfill, verify, and
remove old schema only in a later approved release. Do not run destructive changes
as application startup. Existing `make migrate` is an explicit execution command,
not a dry run or an approval boundary. The caller must use the intended environment.
PostgreSQL migrations serialize via an advisory transaction lock, but this does
not establish a safe lock duration or make every DDL operation reversible.

Application rollback and database rollback are separate decisions. Do not run an
automatic downgrade after a failed deployment. Prefer a forward repair when safe;
restoration can lose writes after the backup and requires an explicit recovery plan.

## Deployment rollback

For GCP Cloud Run: preserve the prior healthy revision and shift traffic back after
confirming its image, configuration and schema compatibility. For Azure Container
Apps: preserve a healthy revision; use the configured revision mode and traffic
weights to restore it. Do not assume a single-revision deployment retains an active
fallback. Internal Azure ingress requires a validation runner with network access.
In both: freeze promotion, identify the failing release, restore the reviewed
revision/traffic, verify health plus user flows, watch errors/latency, and record
the incident. Do not recreate infrastructure or restore a database blindly.
No command in this guide performs a deployment.

## Local baseline and AI hooks

Run `python scripts/performance_baseline.py http://127.0.0.1:8000 --samples 20
--budget-ms 1000` as one command. It performs bounded sequential health GETs,
reports p50/p95 and failures, refuses remote hosts and redirects, and never proves
production capacity. The default budget is illustrative, not a product SLO.

If AI is enabled, from `services/@@API@@` run
`AI_PROVIDER=fake python -m app.infrastructure.ai_evals evals/smoke.json` when the
fake provider is configured. The bundled synthetic dataset checks exact matching.
Replace it with reviewed domain cases; `evaluate(provider, cases, grader)` supports
a local grader through the existing TextProvider port. No Harness runtime import.
Live providers require `--live`, configured credentials and a reviewed dataset;
each run is bounded to 50 requests and 2048 output tokens per request. Actual cost
depends on model/input usage. Reports omit prompts, responses and provider errors.

